{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 1671 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "4f50a4f359173523a9d460143aafeb959d1186ed9014b4e05ffc6c54e094e9cf" :edb/db-before-t 1670 :edb/db-after-t 1671 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534983 50 #inst "2026-10-08T13:10:14.657Z" 13194139534983 true] [17592186175724 1100 "The rate of growth in the number of people $P(t)$ infected with a particular virus  follows the logistic growth model\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} P}{\\mathrm{d} t} =  4P - 0.000\\,01  P^2,  \\qquad P(0) = 5\\,000,\n$$\n\nwhere $t$ is the time, in days, since the virus outbreak began. For which value of $P$ is the number of people infected with the virus growing at the fastest possible rate?" 13194139534983 true] [17592186175724 1100 "The rate of growth in the number of people $P(t)$ infected with a particular virus follows the logistic growth model\n\n$\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} P}{\\mathrm{d} t} = 4P - 0.000\\,01 P^2, \\qquad P(0) = 5\\,000$,\nwhere $t$ is the time, in days, since the virus outbreak began. For which value of $P$ is the number of people infected with the virus growing at the fastest possible rate?" 13194139534983 false] [17592186175724 1145 "A logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\nP' = kP(a-P)\n$$\n\nwhere $k > 0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a > P$ is the carrying capacity.\n\nFor a logistic growth model, the fastest rate of growth occurs when\n\n$$\nP = \\dfrac12a\n$$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3551/Source/Images/16751776874ece803120a91cd197180d.png)\n\nFirst, let's rewrite the right-hand side of the given equation so that it matches the form above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dP}{dt} &= 4P - 0.00001P^{2} \\\\\n&= 0.00001P(400000 - P)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom here, we see that\n\n$$\na = 400\\,000\n$$\n\nHence, the carrying capacity is $400\\,000$, and $P$ grows at its fastest possible rate when\n\n$$\nP = \\dfrac12a = \\dfrac12\\cdot 400\\,000 = 200\\,000\n$$" 13194139534983 false] [17592186175724 1145 "A logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\nP' = kP(a-P),\n$$\n\nwhere $k > 0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a > P$ is the carrying capacity.\n\nFor a logistic growth model, the fastest rate of growth occurs when $P = \\dfrac12a.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045108/assets/9d8f5340bba4d3c407e43f2bc64a4aa2ea986be89b8024baaa4e8ae65e96e5d5.png)\n\nFirst, let's rewrite the right-hand side of the given equation so that it matches the form above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dP}{dt} & =4P-0.000\\,01{P}^{2} \\\\  & =0.000\\,01P(400\\,000-P)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom here, we see that $a = 400\\,000.$ Hence, the carrying capacity is $400\\,000,$ and $P$ grows at its fastest possible rate when \n\n$$\nP = \\dfrac12a = \\dfrac12\\cdot 400\\,000 = 200\\,000.\n$$" 13194139534983 true] [17592186175798 1100 "The population $y(t)$ of some foxes in a forest follows the logistic growth model\n\n$$\ny' =0.5y - 0.002y^2, \\qquad y(0) = 25,\n$$\n\nwhere $t$ is the time, in months, since the observations began. According to this model, what is the maximum possible fox population?" 13194139534983 true] [17592186175798 1100 "The population $y(t)$ of some foxes in a forest follows the logistic growth model\n$y' =0.5y - 0.002y^2, \\qquad y(0) = 25$,\nwhere $t$ is the time, in months, since the observations began. According to this model, what is the maximum possible fox population?" 13194139534983 false] [17592186175798 1145 "A logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\ny' = ky(a-y)\n$$\n\nwhere $k > 0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a > y$ is the carrying capacity.\n\nThe carrying capacity $a$ represents the maximum possible population. A diagram showing a typical logistic growth curve is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3551/Source/Images/23a52670fc85a225d9bd84a9110bd028.png)\n\nFirst, let's rewrite the right-hand side of the given equation so that it matches the form above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny^{′} &= 0.5y - 0.002y^{2} \\\\\n&= 0.002y(250 - y)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom here, we see that $a = 250$. Hence, the carrying capacity is $250$, and we conclude that the number of foxes cannot exceed $250$." 13194139534983 false] [17592186175798 1145 "A logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\ny' = ky(a-y),\n$$\n\nwhere $k > 0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a > y$ is the carrying capacity. \n\nThe carrying capacity $a$ represents the maximum possible population. A diagram showing a typical logistic growth curve is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045108/assets/57f4b604c546daed4f1cb3895191fae0ccac33b5c17aac705f203f0a837c7e69.png)\n\nFirst, let's rewrite the right-hand side of the given equation so that it matches the form above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =0.5y-0.002{y}^{2} \\\\  & =0.002y(250-y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom here, we see that $a = 250.$ Hence, the carrying capacity is $250,$ and we conclude that the number of foxes cannot exceed $250.$" 13194139534983 true] [17592186175799 1100 "Consider the following differential equation for the function $y = y(t)$:\n\n$y' = 2y\\left(60 - \\frac{y}{3} \\right), \\qquad y(0) = 10$\n\nWhat is $\\displaystyle \\lim_{t\\to\\infty}{y(t)}$?" 13194139534983 false] [17592186175799 1100 "Consider the following differential equation for the function $y = y(t)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\ny' = 2y\\left(60 - \\frac{y}{3} \\right), \\qquad y(0) = 10\n$$\n\nWhat is $\\displaystyle \\lim_{t\\to\\infty}{y(t)}?$" 13194139534983 true] [17592186175799 1145 "A logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\ny' = ky(a-y)\n$$\n\nwhere $k > 0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a > y$ is the carrying capacity.\n\nA diagram showing a typical logistic growth curve is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3551/Source/Images/0b183e2ca94cb514e6aec43fd4576cb7.png)\n\nFirst, let's rewrite the right-hand side of the given equation so that it matches the form above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny^{′} &= 2y(60 - \\frac{y}{3}) \\\\\n&= 2y(\\frac{180 - y}{3}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{2y}{3}(180 - y)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom here, we see that $a = 180$. Hence, the carrying capacity is $180$.\n\nFor a given logistic growth model, $y\\to a$ as $t\\to\\infty$. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim_{t\\to\\infty}{y(t)} = 180\n$$" 13194139534983 false] [17592186175799 1145 "A logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\ny' = ky(a-y),\n$$\n\nwhere $k > 0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a > y$ is the carrying capacity.\n\nA diagram showing a typical logistic growth curve is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045108/assets/57f4b604c546daed4f1cb3895191fae0ccac33b5c17aac705f203f0a837c7e69.png)\n\nFirst, let's rewrite the right-hand side of the given equation so that it matches the form above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =2y(60-\\frac{y}{3}) \\\\  & =2y(\\frac{180-y}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2y}{3}(180-y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom here, we see that $a = 180.$ Hence, the carrying capacity is $180.$\n\nFor a given logistic growth model, $y\\to a$ as $t\\to\\infty.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim_{t\\to\\infty}{y(t)} = 180.\n$$" 13194139534983 true] [17592186175881 1020 #edb/ref 17592186324678 13194139534983 true] [17592186175881 1020 #edb/ref 17592186324685 13194139534983 true] [17592186175881 1020 #edb/ref 17592186324695 13194139534983 true] [17592186175881 1020 #edb/ref 17592186324698 13194139534983 true] [17592186175881 1020 #edb/ref 17592186324705 13194139534983 true] [17592186175946 1020 #edb/ref 17592186324622 13194139534983 true] [17592186175946 1020 #edb/ref 17592186324629 13194139534983 true] [17592186175946 1020 #edb/ref 17592186324664 13194139534983 true] [17592186175946 1020 #edb/ref 17592186324671 13194139534983 true] [17592186175946 1020 #edb/ref 17592186324692 13194139534983 true] [17592186175947 1020 #edb/ref 17592186324636 13194139534983 true] [17592186175947 1020 #edb/ref 17592186324643 13194139534983 true] [17592186175947 1020 #edb/ref 17592186324650 13194139534983 true] [17592186175947 1020 #edb/ref 17592186324657 13194139534983 true] [17592186175947 1020 #edb/ref 17592186324688 13194139534983 true] [17592186324622 1017 #edb/ref 17592186324623 13194139534983 true] [17592186324622 1089 "q-118560" 13194139534983 true] [17592186324622 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534983 true] [17592186324622 1100 "The population $y(t)$ of mockingbirds in a forest follows the logistic growth model\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=0.6y-0.0003{y}^{2},\\,y(0)=250,\n$$\n\nwhere $t$ is the time, in months, since the observations began. According to this model, what is the maximum possible population of mockingbirds?" 13194139534983 true] [17592186324622 1113 #uuid "a67cb8d5-18ca-5d11-9dfc-d7a49d4d64b9" 13194139534983 true] [17592186324622 1145 "A logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=ky(a-y),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>y$ is the carrying capacity. \n\nThe carrying capacity $a$ represents the maximum possible population. A diagram showing a typical logistic growth curve is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045108/assets/57f4b604c546daed4f1cb3895191fae0ccac33b5c17aac705f203f0a837c7e69.png)\n\nFirst, let's rewrite the right-hand side of the given equation so that it matches the form above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =0.6y-0.0003{y}^{2} \\\\  & =0.0003y(2\\,000-y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom here, we see that $a=2\\,000.$ Hence, the carrying capacity is $2\\,000,$ and we conclude that the number of mockingbirds cannot exceed $2\\,000.$" 13194139534983 true] [17592186324623 1000 #edb/ref 17592186324624 13194139534983 true] [17592186324623 1000 #edb/ref 17592186324625 13194139534983 true] [17592186324623 1000 #edb/ref 17592186324626 13194139534983 true] [17592186324623 1000 #edb/ref 17592186324627 13194139534983 true] [17592186324623 1000 #edb/ref 17592186324628 13194139534983 true] [17592186324623 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534983 true] [17592186324623 1041 "selection" 13194139534983 true] [17592186324623 1104 #edb/ref 17592186324624 13194139534983 true] [17592186324623 1110 #uuid "66650271-56a9-502c-ab02-30c18494437c" 13194139534983 true] [17592186324624 1034 "2\\,000" 13194139534983 true] [17592186324624 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534983 true] [17592186324624 1111 #uuid "b47c5ce0-9a74-576f-9efe-be9d4e39d0ac" 13194139534983 true] [17592186324625 1034 "2\\,250" 13194139534983 true] [17592186324625 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534983 true] [17592186324625 1111 #uuid "93dd6090-2cf2-536f-be00-96efad2df185" 13194139534983 true] [17592186324626 1034 "600" 13194139534983 true] [17592186324626 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534983 true] [17592186324626 1111 #uuid "371fb11b-71b8-5a8e-97c7-0947148e88e7" 13194139534983 true] [17592186324627 1034 "450" 13194139534983 true] [17592186324627 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534983 true] [17592186324627 1111 #uuid "a08c37d3-d2b1-5b55-a954-6a6de82990a5" 13194139534983 true] [17592186324628 1034 "250" 13194139534983 true] [17592186324628 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534983 true] [17592186324628 1111 #uuid "cdb553d4-f9da-541f-a03d-0f5234ec801d" 13194139534983 true] [17592186324629 1017 #edb/ref 17592186324630 13194139534983 true] [17592186324629 1089 "q-118561" 13194139534983 true] [17592186324629 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534983 true] [17592186324629 1100 "The population $y(t)$ of sloths in a forest follows the logistic growth model\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=0.3y(2-\\frac{y}{300}),\\,y(0)=135,\n$$\n\nwhere $t$ is the time, in years, since the observations began. According to this model, what is the maximum possible sloth population?" 13194139534983 true] [17592186324629 1113 #uuid "fbe1958e-67bc-532f-b098-5d3f40081ea7" 13194139534983 true] [17592186324629 1145 "A logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=ky(a-y),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>y$ is the carrying capacity. \n\nThe carrying capacity $a$ represents the maximum possible population. A diagram showing a typical logistic growth curve is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045108/assets/57f4b604c546daed4f1cb3895191fae0ccac33b5c17aac705f203f0a837c7e69.png)\n\nFirst, let's rewrite the right-hand side of the given equation so that it matches the form above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =0.3y(2-\\frac{y}{300}) \\\\  & =0.3y(\\frac{600-y}{300}) \\\\  & =\\frac{0.3y}{300}(600-y) \\\\  & =0.001y(600-y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom here, we see that $a=600.$ Hence, the carrying capacity is $600,$ and we conclude that the number of sloths cannot exceed $600.$" 13194139534983 true] [17592186324630 1000 #edb/ref 17592186324631 13194139534983 true] [17592186324630 1000 #edb/ref 17592186324632 13194139534983 true] [17592186324630 1000 #edb/ref 17592186324633 13194139534983 true] [17592186324630 1000 #edb/ref 17592186324634 13194139534983 true] [17592186324630 1000 #edb/ref 17592186324635 13194139534983 true] [17592186324630 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534983 true] [17592186324630 1041 "selection" 13194139534983 true] [17592186324630 1104 #edb/ref 17592186324634 13194139534983 true] [17592186324630 1110 #uuid "6122d29a-ea43-57b8-abea-622b420c9fa5" 13194139534983 true] [17592186324631 1034 "800" 13194139534983 true] [17592186324631 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534983 true] [17592186324631 1111 #uuid "ef0cc530-300e-545a-a4e3-14be21f35cc7" 13194139534983 true] [17592186324632 1034 "465" 13194139534983 true] [17592186324632 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534983 true] [17592186324632 1111 #uuid "e46c55d3-1ccc-5291-99d2-151a4328b914" 13194139534983 true] [17592186324633 1034 "1\\,000" 13194139534983 true] [17592186324633 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 131