$\n{y}^{′}=ky(a-y),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>y$ is the carrying capacity. \n\nThe carrying capacity $a$ represents the maximum possible population. A diagram showing a typical logistic growth curve is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045108/assets/57f4b604c546daed4f1cb3895191fae0ccac33b5c17aac705f203f0a837c7e69.png)\n\nFirst, let's rewrite the right-hand side of the given equation so that it matches the form above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =4y(7-\\frac{y}{200}) \\\\  & =4y(\\frac{1\\,400-y}{200}) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{200}y(1\\,400-y) \\\\  & =0.02y(1\\,400-y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom here, we see that $a=1\\,400.$ Hence, the carrying capacity is $1\\,400,$ and we conclude that the number of dolphins cannot exceed $1\\,400.$" 13194139534983 true] [17592186324665 1000 #edb/ref 17592186324666 13194139534983 true] [17592186324665 1000 #edb/ref 17592186324667 13194139534983 true] [17592186324665 1000 #edb/ref 17592186324668 13194139534983 true] [17592186324665 1000 #edb/ref 17592186324669 13194139534983 true] [17592186324665 1000 #edb/ref 17592186324670 13194139534983 true] [17592186324665 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534983 true] [17592186324665 1041 "selection" 13194139534983 true] [17592186324665 1104 #edb/ref 17592186324669 13194139534983 true] [17592186324665 1110 #uuid "58556f93-669f-513a-826a-f8a45ba03e1e" 13194139534983 true] [17592186324666 1034 "70" 13194139534983 true] [17592186324666 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534983 true] [17592186324666 1111 #uuid "801df426-3e35-5c86-b8da-7a5d85a0b3c5" 13194139534983 true] [17592186324667 1034 "43" 13194139534983 true] [17592186324667 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534983 true] [17592186324667 1111 #uuid "ea6513c8-a6f1-53df-81ac-d55079bb4e31" 13194139534983 true] [17592186324668 1034 "400" 13194139534983 true] [17592186324668 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534983 true] [17592186324668 1111 #uuid "6297c3b2-7f6b-53ea-8e1a-b8eb96ca74b9" 13194139534983 true] [17592186324669 1034 "1\\,400" 13194139534983 true] [17592186324669 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534983 true] [17592186324669 1111 #uuid "3f21711b-302e-562a-b710-4bcd13d7fd28" 13194139534983 true] [17592186324670 1034 "200" 13194139534983 true] [17592186324670 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534983 true] [17592186324670 1111 #uuid "b763f903-a7c1-5028-999b-000e7761709c" 13194139534983 true] [17592186324671 1017 #edb/ref 17592186324672 13194139534983 true] [17592186324671 1089 "q-119849" 13194139534983 true] [17592186324671 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534983 true] [17592186324671 1100 "The population $y(t)$ of giraffes on a certain African wildlife reserve follows the logistic growth model\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=17.2y-0.004{y}^{2},\\,y(0)=180,\n$$\n\nwhere $t$ is the time, in years, since the observations began. According to this model, what is the maximum possible population of giraffes?" 13194139534983 true] [17592186324671 1113 #uuid "b4a41fa6-e7d1-54e2-a912-d65af3313e72" 13194139534983 true] [17592186324671 1145 "A logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=ky(a-y),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>y$ is the carrying capacity. \n\nThe carrying capacity $a$ represents the maximum possible population. A diagram showing a typical logistic growth curve is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045108/assets/57f4b604c546daed4f1cb3895191fae0ccac33b5c17aac705f203f0a837c7e69.png)\n\nFirst, let's rewrite the right-hand side of the given equation so that it matches the form above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =17.2y-0.004{y}^{2} \\\\  & =0.004y(4\\,300-y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom here, we see that $a=4\\,300.$ Hence, the carrying capacity is $4\\,300,$ and we conclude that the number of giraffes cannot exceed $4\\,300.$" 13194139534983 true] [1759218632467