m possible number of participants is $250.$" 13194139534983 true] [17592186324693 1000 #edb/ref 17592186324694 13194139534983 true] [17592186324693 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139534983 true] [17592186324693 1041 "field-1" 13194139534983 true] [17592186324693 1104 #edb/ref 17592186324694 13194139534983 true] [17592186324693 1110 #uuid "c4938831-5227-5c39-9a6e-258e3e610976" 13194139534983 true] [17592186324694 1034 "250" 13194139534983 true] [17592186324694 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534983 true] [17592186324694 1111 #uuid "703cc21e-264b-5fe7-9742-04ed82869b48" 13194139534983 true] [17592186324695 1017 #edb/ref 17592186324696 13194139534983 true] [17592186324695 1089 "q-353487" 13194139534983 true] [17592186324695 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534983 true] [17592186324695 1100 "The number $P(t)$ of potential buyers in a market for a new product follows the logistic growth model\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=7200P-3{P}^{2},\\,P(0)=100,\n$$\n\nwhere $t$ is the time, measured in years. For which value of $P$ is the number of potential buyers growing at the fastest possible rate?\n\n$P=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139534983 true] [17592186324695 1113 #uuid "38a73021-b915-534e-9dcf-7cdc220a2098" 13194139534983 true] [17592186324695 1145 "A logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n{P}^{′}=kP(a-P),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>P$ is the carrying capacity.\n\nFor a logistic growth model, the fastest rate of growth occurs when $P=\\frac{1}{2}a.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045108/assets/9019b26b417c757bbfab79afb39379c1fc7a892bc44e934c6f23c339deacfd68.png)\n\nFirst, let's rewrite the right-hand side of the given equation so that it matches the form above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dP}{dt} & =7200P-3{P}^{2} \\\\  & =3P(2400-P)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom here, we see that $a=2400.$ Hence, the carrying capacity is $2400,$ and $P$ grows at its fastest possible 